## Live learnings — do not re-derive, do not re-chase

**Metadata / Raft**
- The apply IS the allocator: the CREATE/MKDIR command's ino is a hint
  (`alloc_hint_or_next`), honored only if ≥ watermark and unoccupied;
  hashed-create / cross-group-mkdir txns CAS the ALLOC key; log-path allocs
  are BUSY under a pending intent and bump the key version
  (`alloc_key_claim`). Callers read the ino back from the dentry.
- Same-parent concurrent dir ops no longer conflict on the parent row
 (§7.2 reductions, below). A dir-op txn still lands STALE/BUSY on a real
 conflict — same name, same child row, emptiness witness moved — and the
 client retries (`stale_retryable`: CREATE/UNLINK/LINK/RENAME_AT).
- **Parent row / dseq / dir-lane are commutative reductions (§7.2, Sep 21):**
 `EFS_TXN_REDUCE_INO` (signed nlink/nents delta, times MAX, used_shards
 OR, parent SET) and `EFS_TXN_REDUCE_ADD` (dseq +1) fold into whatever the
 key holds at RESOLVE (`fold_reduce`, dispatch by DATA key kind) and bump
 its version. A pending reduce and an EXCL/GUARD on one key are mutually
 BUSY; the log path probes before an unversioned DEL (`dir_txn_busy`,
 `efs_txn_key_busy`) and returns BUSY; dseq GUARDs compare the observed
 VALUE (`efs_txn_dseq_observe`), never the version. Never reintroduce a
 full-image EXCL PUT of a parent row — it was the Sep 20 lost update
 (`test-dir.0-0` nlink=2 with 3 children, rmdir EIO forever). Client
 retries STALE for CREATE/UNLINK/LINK/RENAME_AT. Repro/gate:
 `tests/stress/same_parent_storm.sh` (parent must end `children=0
 nlink=2`, rmdir OK). The old flat ~140 ops/s was one AppendEntries
 per op (a nested proposer broadcast under `h->mu`). Sep 27:
 `results/measure/20260927-211953-samedir-rate` is 333/1385/1241.
 Do not put every proposer on the fsync hold (idle mkdir 7.1 → 11.9 ms).
- **Never scan a whole shard (2-byte prefix) in the apply path.**
  `efs_txn_resolve`/`efs_txn_drop` did (0.7–1.1 s per RESOLVE on the
  root's shard, every replica, under the KV lock and `h->mu`) → the
  bimodal 2 ms / 1 s root mkdir, 400 ms `host_wait_applied` BUSY, 10.4 s
  client backoff, retried committed UNLINK → ENOENT. Fixed `165e779`
  (`txn_scan_kinds`: three 3-byte prefixes). Bound every scan by kind.
  **Latent hazard, now the open I16 item:** a BUSY from the apply-wait
  deadline makes the client retry a non-idempotent op (LINK/UNLINK/
  MKDIR/RENAME) that may already have committed → EEXIST/ENOENT/EIO.
- **A txn record with no coordinator behind it is permanent poison until
  recovery removes it** (a coordinator BUSY on its own DECIDE/RESOLVE
  wait left INTENT/GUARD/REDUCE behind; an EXCL intent on a shard's
  ALLOC key made every log-path create/mkdir there BUSY; 105 orphans,
  `results/measure/20260921-w8-orphans`). Since `9534e53`/`3291c6d` the
  group leader's GC thread (`host_txn_recover_pass`) scans a shard's
  three txn-record prefixes once its PREPARE-apply mark is 5 s old and
  DECIDEs/RESOLVEs (PROTO = it was COMMITted); logs `txn-recover`.
  Diagnose with `tests/tools/kv_intents` on a COPY of `mdraft/kv`. A
  `(retry next pass)` line every 5 s = coordinator group without a leader.
  Since the Sep 26 finisher, `host_txn_commit` also queues a txn whose
  DECIDE/RESOLVE wait returned BUSY; the finisher thread reads the
  decision and proposes RESOLVE (retries BUSY for 10 s). `raft-obs`
  prints `fin_q` / `fin_done` / `fin_drop`. A stall longer than 400 ms
  is still the compaction (`apply_max`, `persist_max` ~1 ms) — W13.
- **Never run KV prefix scans in a loop on a server thread.** One
 `efs_kv_scan_prefix` = one block `pread` per LSM segment whose key
 range can hold the prefix (all of them before Sep 26; now L0s plus
 one or two L1s), under the LSM mutex the apply path needs; the first recovery
  sweep (1 536/s per leader, 3 % CPU) put every metadata op at ~100 ms
  (`results/measure/20260921-w8-orphans/sweep-regression.txt`). Lock
  hold, not CPU. Check `python3 tests/measure/md_latency.py` (reference
  medians 6.1/4.0/6.2/0.3/1.5/5.2 ms since `84a2a55`) after any change
  that adds background KV work.
- `efs_kv_scan_prefix` callback returning **>0 means batch-full**, not
 error. The reaper (`lane-sweep`, `rsv_purge`) misread it for weeks —
 no lane with >64 chunks was ever swept. Check this in any new scan user.
- **Lane shards straddle both groups** (`lane_shard = ish + lane × odd
 stride`): every odd lane of an inode is in the other group. Anything the
 anchor-group leader drives per lane (reaper `LANE_SWEEP`) must forward
 foreign-group commands (`host_gc_propose` → `host_remote_cmd`, Sep 20);
 leader-only made every inode with an active odd lane unreapable, and the
 retry stream (92 % of the log, 33 entries/s per group, idle cluster)
 queued every client commit behind it.
 **Before any latency or metadata measurement: `commit` flat for 30 s on
 both groups, and `tests/tools/raft_log_tail.py` on the leader's
 `raft.log` shows no command dominating.**
- **The ~100 ms metadata floor (mkdir med 103 ms, append+close 170) was a
 wakeup bug, not fsync:** raft_host's per-peer sender threads all waited on
 one shared condvar with `cond_signal`, so a queued AE/AE-reply usually
 woke the wrong sender and left at the next 50 ms heartbeat, 0–50 ms per
 hop. Per-peer `tx[].cv` since Sep 20: mkdir med **7.2 ms**, create+close
 6.7, append+close 9.0, stat 0.4 (`results/perf/20260921-md-latency.txt`).
 A raft commit costs ~2–6 ms here; a median far above that on an idle
 cluster is a scheduling/wakeup bug — strace the FOLLOWER (acks every
 other heartbeat = this class) before blaming fsync.
- **Handlers never take `h->mu` to read raft state or to wait** (`4eb1419`).
 The pump publishes commit/applied/term/role/leader/read-round as atomics
 (`host_publish_view`); readers use the view and sleep on `applied_cv`
 under `cv_mu`; `h->mu` only to propose or read_begin. Before, 30+
 handlers per cycle sat on the pump's mutex, it lost its heartbeat
 cadence and both groups re-elected 4× in 50 s under the 9-host suite.
 Waiters sleep in per-waiter slots (`host_waiter_sleep`) and the pump
 signals only the satisfied ones (`host_waiters_wake`, Sep 26); a
 `cond_broadcast` to 150 handlers per cycle was ~20 % of efsd in
 futex/spinlock. Any new wait-for-view goes through a slot, not a
 shared condvar.
- **One KV WAL fsync per pump cycle** (`sync_hold` around drain+tick,
 release before `persist_applied`, `84a2a55`): per-put fsync was 0.5 ms
 per applied entry under `h->mu` on every replica. Spec: the Raft log is
 the durability boundary; the KV must be durable only before the saved
 applied index advances. No per-apply fsync; no snapshot past the KV's
 durable point.
- **The compactor's L0 back-pressure wait is on the pump (Sep 29
  04:27Z trace).** `kv_maybe_flush_locked` waits on `l->cv` while
  `n_l0 + 16 > 64`; the caller is the apply path under `h->mu`, so a
  1.8 GB range-0 rewrite (24 s) is a 24 s pump hold: no heartbeats,
  `host_wait_applied` 400 ms timeouts (`apply-sleep`), REPORT BUSY,
  client fsync EIO. The compactor's segment `fsync` is the 100 ms mode
  (its own 200–300 MB of dirty pages); the pump's Raft-log `fsync` is
  0.4 ms. A snapshot export is no longer in the picture (W22.1).
  Compaction shape is a design decision (D9/D10) — ask.
- **L1 compaction is a background thread (W13, Sep 26).** An L0 still
 spans every shard, so each cycle rewrites all of L1 (~760 MiB, ~2 s
 on the live table). The merge uses private segment opens and holds
 no `l->mu` and no `h->mu`; the pump only swaps the manifest. A
 segment file is unlinked when its pin count hits 0, so a pinned
 view (and a lookup pread) keeps the old file. `apply_max` during
 those 2 s rewrites was 68 ms. The write path blocks only if `n_l0`
 hits 64 (`kv-compact: backpressure`). `kv_compact_locked` remains
 the synchronous path for tests and for a thread that failed to
 start. Do not put compaction back under the pump. Partitioned
 flush is the unread follow-on. A point get still pins the segment
 and preads outside `l->mu` (`5d3e603`).
- **A Raft proposal is identified by `(index, term)`, never by index
  (I17 root cause, fixed `46d54e6`):** the apply ring matched by index;
  after a leader change the truncated entry's index is reused and the
  old leader's coordinator read the new entry's OK as "DECIDE COMMIT
  applied" → RESOLVE COMMIT on one shard, recovery ABORT on the rest =
  ino 62991 (nlink=3 nents=1, 0 dentries), ino 31264 (dentry → missing
  row), and the forced repro `tests/measure/i17_leader_freeze.sh` on
  `84a2a55` (`results/measure/20260922-042153-i17-leader-freeze`).
 Now: term mismatch → NOT_PRIMARY, ring slot overwritten → BUSY, never
 OK; `raft-obs` prints `arc_term_miss=`. Any new wait-for-apply must go
 through `host_wait_verdict` / `host_wait_settled`, not a bare
 `host_wait_applied` + ring read. Pre-fix leftovers on 19810 stay
 half-applied (no repair tool); dump with `tests/tools/kv_dir_dump` on
 a KV COPY (fcstor004/005 hold every shard) before blaming new code.
- **A retried dir op is answered from its op-id window (I16, `43bdf6a`):**
 the window's shard is the dentry shard of the destination name, never
 the coordinator (randomized) or the parent shard (may be the other
 group). Record the verdict in the same txn (`host_prep_opid`,
 `EFS_TXN_REDUCE_OPID`) or the same KV batch (`opid_item`) as the op —
 never as a separate write, and never make the apply return the
 pre-check error (EEXIST/ENOENT) itself; the leader's pre-check runs
 after `host_opid_replay`. The client holds one in-flight slot across
 the whole `rpc_send_recv_*` retry loop so every retry is byte-identical;
 a new RPC = a new seq. Do not add a client-side "stop retrying BUSY".
- **A hintless NOT_PRIMARY (`primary_id == 0`) is an election in
 progress, not an error** (Sep 23): a stale leader that just stepped down
 answers NOT_PRIMARY with no hint for up to a heartbeat or two; the client
 used to return `EFS_ERR_NOT_PRIMARY` at once, `efs_client_stat_ino` maps
 every RPC failure to NOT_FOUND, and the FUSE `*_at` helpers map that to
 ENOENT — so `mkdir` on a dir that had just committed returned ENOENT
 (`ll_mkdir` → `lookup_fill(new_ino)` GETATTR; freeze run 2,
 `results/measure/20260923-025903-i17-leader-freeze`). Now
  `rpc_send_recv_shard/_dual` back off like BUSY. **Sep 29: the steady
  source of hintless NOT_PRIMARY on a stable cluster is
  `host_read_inode_lanes` on a single-group host** (a foreign lane's
  `host_read_index` returns `!v.has` → hint −1, after the op committed:
  setattr, rename, utimens, unlink, link, getattr callers). It cost
  50 ms per hit on the skip-the-voter client and 16× → 10 s EBUSY
  before (the 810 `exhausted` lines in fstor007's ecopy log). Fix is
  START-HERE §1b 20:55Z item 2 (hint a dual host, check before
  propose). An ENOENT/EIO from a
 FUSE dir op whose target exists is a reply-path RPC failure first —
 grep `fuse: mkdir ... but getattr` / `inode-rpc:` in `fuse.log` before
 suspecting metadata.
- **A COMMITted directory entry is visible before RESOLVE** (Sep 23,
 `lookup_tx`): handler lookup and resolve read through an EXCL intent
 whose coordinator has COMMITted (`efs_meta_apply_lookup_tx` /
 `resolve_tx`), same rule as `get_inode_tx` for the inode row. The
 apply path stays on the plain read. A negative lookup pays one extra
 local get of the intent key; the coordinator is asked only when an
 intent exists. `rmdir`/`unlink` map `EFS_ERR_BUSY` to EBUSY. The 16-
 attempt client budget (~10 s) is shorter than a freeze's recovery
 (16 s on shard 1504); do not widen it.
- **A queued posix test cannot time out** (`posix_suite.py` stamped the
 clock at submit until `a683def`): every 9-host number before Sep 21
 22:00 was that artefact plus real stalls. Compare only against later runs.
- Rolling restarts trigger ~5 min of group elections afterwards: peer
 conns are RDMA (`peer_pool.c`), a restarted node's QPs die on its peers
 with `transport retry counter exceeded` and block senders. Pre-existing,
 converges; not the flow-control bug (that one is fixed).
- Raft catch-up: follower dedupes same index+term entries; leader keeps
  one AE batch in flight per peer (`ae_inflight`). Before this, 005
  rewrote the same 128-entry window ~30×/s with an fsync each and never
  caught up. A restarted leader re-exports the snapshot blob on demand;
  `disk_save_snap` accepts skip-ahead (InstallSnapshot onto a behind
  follower). Salt is replicated via `EFS_MD_CMD_SALT`.
- **Snapshot install must be a sorted diff.** `efs_kv_group_import`
  used to scan every local key against the whole image and then PUT
  every key, on the pump under `h->mu`. On the first chunked install
  that held fcstor003 and fcstor004 for hours (`incoming_has` in
  `kv_snap.c`). A live group that already matches the image writes
  nothing. Do not put that scan back. The export itself stays on the
  GC thread; `apply_max` during a 386 MB export was 0. While that
 export is in progress `send_snap` returns BUSY; `send_ae` must still
 emit an empty AppendEntries or the peer campaigns and steps the
 leader down (Sep 27 group 2, term +200, 9-host mkdir EBUSY).
- `host_pump` ticks are wall-clocked (`HOST_TICK_US`), never per iteration
  (iteration-counted ticks caused election storms). The network inbox path
  must never take `h->mu` (eventfd kick, not condvar). Forwarded-command
  replies read the apply-result ring at the command's OWN index
  (`host_propose_wait_idx`), never `efs_raft_applied()`.
- Server partial-commit on STALE in REPORT (skip stale recs, commit the
  rest, reply STALE) + client bulk repull/replay of only moved chunks
  (`stale_repull_replay`) is what broke the shared-file fsync livelock.
  Raft was never the bottleneck there (commit==applied throughout).
- `EFS_MSG_INODE_LOOKUP` reply carries the full row; LOOKUP_PATH batches
  ≤64 ancestors; readdir cursor is `(src, name)`, not `after_ino`.

**Client / FUSE**
- Timeouts stay **0** (entry/attr/negative). `notify_inval_*` from a
  request handler deadlocks — queue after reply on a dedicated thread.
  `FOPEN_DIRECT_IO` on every open; libfuse 3.10.2 cannot emit
  `PARALLEL_DIRECT_WRITES` or `FUSE_MAX_PAGES`.
- `dcache_image_current`: a cached whole image is served only when the map
  names the same generation (`tg != 0 && tg == base_gen`) or the slot is
  explicitly dirty/unpublished. `snap_seq`/`object_seq` order concurrent
  PUTs of one chunk; `dcache_install_image` overlays in-flight dirty
  ranges. Truncate drops rdcache + clean dcache for the dropped chunks.
- A read whose layout pull fails must FAIL, not zero-fill (I9). The old
  1/s rate-limited `pull_layout_miss` returned 38054 zero 1 MiB reads in
  1.36 s and looked like 4 GiB/s.
- `adopt_rpc_inode` drops dcache only on SHRINK; dropping on
  same-size/newer-mtime discarded the only replay source of an in-flight
  O_APPEND object (`concurrent_appends` lost lines).
- `.fuse_hidden*` is libfuse's silly-rename (unlink/rename of an open
  file); readdir hides the prefix; `hard_remove` was rejected.
- Client on-CPU under writes is blake3 in `hash_write_fragments`; the wall
  is off-CPU (fsync/REPORT). Optimizing blake3 does not move the number.

**RDMA**
- Auto/RDMA first-`mkdir` hang on an EMPTY table: root cause was the client
  closing a pooled conn's TCP side-channel (fd recycled) so the server
  reaped the QP. Pool identity fix is in tree (`test_conn_fd`), live gate
  needs an EMPTY table — `tests/rdma_first_inode.sh` is one (private
  3-node cluster on fcstor007, ports 19950–19952; W10 recommends this,
  not a wipe of 19810). RNR-NAK / recv-buffer hypothesis is
  **dead**. A populated table drove millions of creates over RDMA
  with 0 errors. The Sep 27 live switch of 19810 failed the 9-host
  posix (many-op tests hit 15 s; `apply_max` under 1 ms) and was
  rolled back. Cause, fixed in tree and not rolled: the shared recv
  poller slept 100 ms after acking a CQ event, and every SEND called
  `ibv_query_qp` plus a sysfs read (~380 µs raft RTT vs ~15 µs TCP).
  Private 100-mkdir afterward was 642 ms vs 507 ms TCP. Default
  stays TCP. Do not debug that on the live cluster.

**Harness / measurement**
- IO-500 harness detaches `prterun` (ssh timeout killed the driver);
  `ior-easy|hard-write|verify` run IOR directly for cold verifies (the
  io500 driver deletes its data). Kill hung ranks with `pkill -9 -x io500`
  then remount; never gdb-attach a rank through a timeout'd ssh.
- A 4-client fio storm can drop raft heartbeats (`rc=-15`); recovery is a
  keep-storage efsd bounce, not a wipe. Two 4-client 9×2g jobs exceed the
  400 s ssh budget — do not raise it.
- `dd if=/dev/zero` skips PUTs (all-zero chunks) — use a non-zero source.
- posix harness reuses an existing mount (`ensure_mounted`); it remounts
  only on a transport mismatch. 9-way posix hits the 385 s python cap and
  reports `[None]` for never-ran tests (W8) — not 60 bugs.
- Wait for the cluster to be apply-idle (`commit == applied` on all
  voters) before starting a measurement; a run started during catch-up
  measures the catch-up.

## Habit

After any milestone: update START-HERE (§1a item state, §1b handoff), fix
the fact here that changed, and append the narrative to
`docs/project-history.md` under a dated heading. Commit result directories
that a rule or START-HERE cites; delete the rest.
